window.GD_ASSIST={"lessons":[{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/00-overview.html","t":"Welcome — and how to learn this","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: learn like a cook, not like a photocopier · 📐 A one-minute preview of the whole track · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Every model you will ever build — a spam filter, a face recognizer, ChatGPT — runs on two ideas from this track: lists of numbers (vectors) and grids of numbers (matrices). This lesson shows you the objects you are about to master and, just as important, the way to learn them so they actually stick."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/01-vectors.html","t":"Vectors: the atoms of ML","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: a set of walking directions · 📐 The concept, built up in small steps · 🛠️ The same thing in code · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A vector is one object wearing three costumes at once: a list of numbers, an arrow in space, and a point. Get comfortable switching between all three and you have grasped the single most-used idea in machine learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/02-vector-operations.html","t":"Vector operations & geometry","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: two trips, one destination · 📐 The concept, built up in small steps · 🛠️ The same thing in code · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"There are only two things you can ever do to vectors: add them, and scale them. Astonishingly, all of linear algebra — and the entire training loop of a neural network — is built from just these two moves."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/03-dot-product.html","t":"The dot product: length, angle, projection","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: how much do two arrows agree? · 📐 The concept, built up in small steps · 🛠️ The same thing in code · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One operation eats two vectors and returns a single number — and that number secretly encodes length, angle, and similarity all at once. It is the most-run computation in machine learning: every neural network is, at heart, a pile of dot products."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/04-span-basis.html","t":"Linear combinations, span & basis","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: how far can two ingredients take you? · 📐 The concept, built up in small steps · 🛠️ The same thing in code · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Given a handful of vectors, which points can you reach by scaling and adding them? The answer — and the idea of a “smallest set that reaches everything” — is the backbone of dimension, coordinates, and later PCA."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/05-matrices-as-maps.html","t":"Matrices as linear transformations","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: move the two corner-posts, drag all of space · 📐 The concept, built up in small steps · 🛠️ The same thing in code · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Stop thinking of a matrix as a grid of numbers. It’s a function that moves space — and it’s completely described by where it sends the basis vectors. This single mental model makes matrices, neural-network layers, and everything after click into place."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/06-matrix-multiplication.html","t":"Matrix multiplication = doing one transformation after another","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: chaining two machines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The rule for multiplying matrices looks arbitrary when you first meet it. It isn't. It's forced on us by one simple demand: the product of two matrices must be the single matrix that does what both transformations do, one after the other."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/07-determinant.html","t":"The determinant: area, volume, invertibility","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: a zoom dial for area · 🧭 Building it up in small steps · 🛠️ In code: three lines, three facts · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One number, squeezed out of a square matrix, that tells you how much the matrix stretches space — and whether the stretch can ever be undone. It quietly ties together area, orientation, linear independence, and invertibility."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/08-eigenvectors.html","t":"Eigenvectors & eigenvalues","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: the arrows the wind blows straight · 🧭 How to actually find them · 🛠️ In code: let NumPy do the algebra · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Almost every vector gets knocked off its line by a transformation. A precious few don’t — they only stretch. Those special directions, and their stretch factors, reveal the hidden skeleton of a matrix, and they are the mathematical heart of PCA."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/09-probability-bayes.html","t":"Probability foundations & Bayes’ rule","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: a detective updating a hunch · 🧭 Bayes’ rule: flipping the condition · 🛠️ In code: Bayes in four lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Machine learning is reasoning under uncertainty, and probability is its grammar. We build it from three simple axioms up to Bayes’ rule — the formula for updating beliefs when new evidence arrives, and the backbone of countless ML models."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/10-random-variables.html","t":"Random variables & distributions","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: a number that hasn’t happened yet · 🧭 Continuous values: density, not mass · 🛠️ In code: sample, then watch it converge · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"To do math with chance, we attach numbers to outcomes — that’s a random variable — and describe how those numbers are spread out — that’s a distribution. These two ideas turn “uncertainty” into something you can compute, sample, and learn from."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/11-expectation-variance.html","t":"Expectation, variance & covariance","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: the balance point and the typical wobble · 🧭 Variance: how far things typically stray · 🔗 Covariance: do two things move together? · 🛠️ In code: two running sums, then a matrix · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A distribution can be complicated, but three numbers capture most of what you need: where it’s centered, how spread out it is, and — for two variables — whether they move together. These summaries are the vocabulary of every loss function and the gateway to PCA."},{"f":"lessons/track-1-linear-algebra-probability/12-gaussian-mle.html","t":"The Gaussian, maximum likelihood, and the bridge to ML","tn":"Linear Algebra Probability","k":"1","x":"💡 Intuition: which bell curve best explains the data? · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One bell-shaped curve shows up everywhere in nature and machine learning. Pair it with one principle — pick the parameters that make your data most probable — and you've derived, from first principles, why we minimise squared error."},{"f":"lessons/track-10-capstones/00-capstone-namegen.html","t":"Capstone A: ship a from-scratch GPT","tn":"Capstones","k":"10","x":"💡 What you’re building · 🛠️ The model: a Transformer that invents names · 🛠️ The server: a real API · 📐 Deploying it: laptop to public URL · 🔑 Key takeaways · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice & extensions","s":"Everything you built, deployed. Take the character-level Transformer from Track 5, train it to invent names, wrap it in a real web service, and put it online — a complete, professional, real-time app you can push to GitHub today."},{"f":"lessons/track-10-capstones/01-capstone-ddlab.html","t":"Capstone B: reproduce a classic, live","tn":"Capstones","k":"10","x":"💡 What you’re building · 📐 The result it reproduces · 🛠️ The science as a tested module · 📐 Deploying it · 🔑 Key takeaways · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice & extensions","s":"Turn a published result into an interactive web app. The double-descent reproduction from Track 8 becomes a live lab where anyone can drag sliders and watch the interpolation spike appear and vanish — a deployable scientific artifact."},{"f":"lessons/track-10-capstones/02-capstone-gridworld.html","t":"Capstone C: a live reinforcement-learning agent","tn":"Capstones","k":"10","x":"💡 What you’re building · 🛠️ The agent: Q-learning that trains on demand · 📐 Tests that prove it learns, and deployment · 🔑 Key takeaways · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice & extensions","s":"Make learning visible. Deploy the Q-learning agent from Track 7 as a web app where visitors click “Train” and watch the value heatmap and policy arrows sharpen in real time — the most dynamic capstone, and a perfect teaching tool."},{"f":"lessons/track-11-llms/00-tokenization-bpe.html","t":"Tokenization at scale: Byte-Pair Encoding","tn":"Llms","k":"11","x":"💡 Intuition: inventing shorthand as you go · 🧠 The idea in small steps · 🛠️ From scratch: learn merges in a loop · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A language model never sees words or even letters — it sees tokens , integer IDs for chunks of text. Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) is the clever trick that decides what those chunks are: start from raw bytes and repeatedly glue together the pair that shows up most, until common chunks each get their own token."},{"f":"lessons/track-11-llms/01-scaling-laws.html","t":"Scaling laws & the training recipe","tn":"Llms","k":"11","x":"💡 Concept: loss follows a power law in scale · 🛠️ From scratch: fit a scaling law and extrapolate · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Why did the field bet billions on making models bigger? Because loss falls with scale in a startlingly predictable way — a power law you can fit on cheap small models and extrapolate. This is the equation behind “just scale it up.”"},{"f":"lessons/track-11-llms/02-lora-finetuning.html","t":"Fine-tuning & LoRA","tn":"Llms","k":"11","x":"💡 Concept: the update is low-rank, so factor it · 🛠️ From scratch: LoRA matches full fine-tuning · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"You can’t retrain a billion-parameter model on your laptop — but you can adapt one. LoRA freezes the giant pretrained weights and learns a tiny low-rank update, matching full fine-tuning at a fraction of the cost. It’s how most custom LLMs are actually built."},{"f":"lessons/track-11-llms/03-efficient-inference.html","t":"Efficient inference: KV-cache & quantization","tn":"Llms","k":"11","x":"💡 The KV-cache: don’t recompute the past · 📐 Quantization: fewer bits per weight · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Training gets the headlines, but serving a model to users is where most of the compute is spent. Two techniques make it affordable: the KV-cache (never recompute the past) and quantization (store weights in fewer bits). Both are simple, and you’ll build them."},{"f":"lessons/track-11-llms/04-rag.html","t":"Retrieval-augmented generation & in-context learning","tn":"Llms","k":"11","x":"💡 Foundation: in-context learning · 📐 Retrieval by similarity in embedding space · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A model’s weights are a frozen snapshot of its training data — it can’t know your documents or today’s news. RAG fixes this by giving the model an external memory it searches at question time. It’s the architecture behind most real-world LLM applications."},{"f":"lessons/track-12-classical-ml/00-linear-logistic-regression.html","t":"Linear & Logistic Regression: where machine learning begins","tn":"Classical Ml","k":"12","x":"💡 Intuition: the line of best fit, and bending it into a probability · 🛠️ From scratch: both models are a few lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Before the fancy models, there are two you must know in your bones: fit a straight line to predict a number, and bend that line through an S-curve to predict a probability. They're the simplest models that work — and secretly, they're single neurons."},{"f":"lessons/track-12-classical-ml/01-knn.html","t":"k-Nearest Neighbors","tn":"Classical Ml","k":"12","x":"💡 Concept: classify by neighbourhood vote · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Before deep learning, a whole toolbox of classical models solved — and still solves — most real-world ML problems. We start with the simplest idea of all: to classify something, look at what’s nearby."},{"f":"lessons/track-12-classical-ml/02-decision-trees.html","t":"Decision trees","tn":"Classical Ml","k":"12","x":"💡 Concept: split to reduce impurity · 🛠️ From scratch: CART, and the overfitting knob · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A model that thinks in questions: “is this feature above a threshold?”, again and again, splitting the data into ever-purer groups. Trees are interpretable on their own — and the building block of the two most powerful classical models."},{"f":"lessons/track-12-classical-ml/03-random-forests.html","t":"Random forests","tn":"Classical Ml","k":"12","x":"💡 Concept: bagging + random features · 🛠️ From scratch: a forest beats a deep tree · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One tree overfits. So grow a whole forest of them — each on a different random slice of the data — and let them vote. Averaging many unstable trees cancels their errors, giving one of the most reliable off-the-shelf models in all of ML."},{"f":"lessons/track-12-classical-ml/04-gradient-boosting.html","t":"Gradient boosting","tn":"Classical Ml","k":"12","x":"💡 Concept: fit the residual, again and again · 🛠️ From scratch: gradient boosting in a loop · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Random forests build trees in parallel and average them. Boosting builds them one at a time, each fixing the last one’s mistakes. Done right, it’s gradient descent in function space — and the reigning champion of tabular machine learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-12-classical-ml/05-svm.html","t":"Support Vector Machines & the kernel trick","tn":"Classical Ml","k":"12","x":"💡 Concept: the maximum-margin separator · 📐 The kernel trick: linear power, curved boundaries · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Of all the lines that separate two classes, which is best? The SVM answers: the one with the widest margin. And with one elegant trick, the same linear method draws curved boundaries — the idea that dominated ML before deep learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-13-information-theory/00-entropy.html","t":"Entropy: the measure of uncertainty","tn":"Information Theory","k":"13","x":"💡 Concept: surprise, and its average · 🛠️ From scratch: entropy in one line · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"“Cross-entropy loss” and “KL divergence” turn up all over machine learning — in every classifier, in VAEs, in RLHF. This track builds the theory behind those names, and it all starts with one quantity: entropy, the amount of surprise in a distribution."},{"f":"lessons/track-13-information-theory/01-cross-entropy.html","t":"Cross-entropy: the loss you’ve always used","tn":"Information Theory","k":"13","x":"💡 Concept: the cost of a wrong belief · 📐 Why minimizing it = maximum likelihood · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Every classifier and language model you’ve trained minimized “cross-entropy loss.” Now you’ll see exactly what it measures — the cost of using the wrong distribution — and why minimizing it is the same as maximum likelihood."},{"f":"lessons/track-13-information-theory/02-kl-divergence.html","t":"KL divergence: distance between distributions","tn":"Information Theory","k":"13","x":"💡 Concept: the extra bits of a wrong code · 📐 Why the asymmetry matters · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"You met KL divergence twice already — as the VAE’s regularizer and RLHF’s penalty — without deriving it. Here it is: the number of extra bits you pay for believing the wrong distribution, and the true target behind cross-entropy training."},{"f":"lessons/track-13-information-theory/03-mutual-information.html","t":"Mutual information: how much X tells you about Y","tn":"Information Theory","k":"13","x":"💡 Concept: shared information · 🛠️ From scratch: estimating MI by binning · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Correlation measures only straight-line relationships and is blind to everything else. Mutual information measures any dependence — the true amount, in bits, that one variable reveals about another. It’s the honest way to ask “are these two things related?”"},{"f":"lessons/track-13-information-theory/04-compression.html","t":"Compression: prediction and compression are the same thing","tn":"Information Theory","k":"13","x":"💡 Concept: give frequent symbols short codes · 📐 A model is a compressor · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Entropy isn’t just a number — it’s a hard limit on how small data can be squeezed. Building an optimal code from scratch reveals a profound idea: a model that predicts well is exactly a model that compresses well. That equivalence is the deep reason learning works."},{"f":"lessons/track-14-safety/00-alignment-problem.html","t":"The alignment problem & specification gaming","tn":"Safety","k":"14","x":"💡 Concept: capability is not alignment · 🛠️ From scratch: an agent games its reward · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"You’ve spent this whole curriculum making models more capable . This final track asks the harder question: how do we make them do what we actually want ? It begins with a humbling fact — a capable optimizer will exploit any gap between what you reward and what you mean."},{"f":"lessons/track-14-safety/01-goodhart-reward-hacking.html","t":"Goodhart’s law & reward hacking","tn":"Safety","k":"14","x":"💡 Concept: the over-optimization curve · 🛠️ From scratch: watch true value collapse · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Optimize a good-enough proxy gently and everything improves. Optimize it too hard and it turns on you: the metric keeps climbing while the thing you actually cared about collapses. This over-optimization curve is one of the most important pictures in AI safety."},{"f":"lessons/track-14-safety/02-interpretability.html","t":"Mechanistic interpretability","tn":"Safety","k":"14","x":"💡 Concept: read the representation with a probe · 🛠️ From scratch: probe a hidden layer · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A trained network is a black box of millions of numbers. Interpretability is the science of prying it open — and the simplest tool, a linear probe, already reveals something profound: you can read a concept straight out of a hidden layer."},{"f":"lessons/track-14-safety/03-evaluations.html","t":"Evaluations & red-teaming","tn":"Safety","k":"14","x":"💡 Concept: the average hides the worst case · 🛠️ From scratch: disaggregate, then red-team · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A single accuracy number is a comforting lie. A model can score 90% overall and fail completely for a group of people who matter. Safety evaluation means refusing the average — disaggregating, stress-testing, and actively hunting for the failures a benchmark hides."},{"f":"lessons/track-14-safety/04-adversarial-robustness.html","t":"Adversarial robustness","tn":"Safety","k":"14","x":"💡 Concept: attack along the gradient · 🛠️ From scratch: fool a classifier · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A perturbation too small for a human to notice can turn a confident classifier’s answer into nonsense. Adversarial examples reveal that “high accuracy” and “robust understanding” are not the same thing — and you can craft one with the very gradients you used to train."},{"f":"lessons/track-15-cnns-vision/00-convolution.html","t":"Convolution: the operation that sees","tn":"Cnns Vision","k":"15","x":"💡 Intuition: a stencil you drag across the picture · 🛠️ From scratch: the whole operation in ten lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"An MLP treats an image as a flat list of pixels and throws away the one thing that matters most — that nearby pixels belong together. Convolution keeps that structure, and with it a network can learn to see edges, textures, and eventually cats. It starts with one small grid of shared weights."},{"f":"lessons/track-15-cnns-vision/01-pooling-stride.html","t":"Pooling & stride: shrink the picture, keep the point","tn":"Cnns Vision","k":"15","x":"💡 Intuition: summarize each little patch with one number · 🛠️ From scratch: pooling in a few lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Convolution finds features but leaves the map as big as the image. Pooling summarizes each little patch with a single number — making the network smaller and faster, and giving it a helpful blindness to exactly where a feature sits."},{"f":"lessons/track-15-cnns-vision/02-convnet.html","t":"A ConvNet from scratch — and the filters it teaches itself","tn":"Cnns Vision","k":"15","x":"💡 Intuition: an assembly line for pictures · 🛠️ The one new piece of training: the filter's gradient · 🔎 What did it learn? Look inside · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Convolution plus pooling plus a small classifier, trained by the same gradient descent from Track 4, is a complete convolutional network. We build one in pure NumPy, train it on raw pixels, and then open it up to see the edge detectors it invented on its own."},{"f":"lessons/track-15-cnns-vision/03-augmentation.html","t":"Data augmentation: more training data, for free","tn":"Cnns Vision","k":"15","x":"💡 Intuition: same meaning, new picture · 🛠️ From scratch: a random augmentation · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The cheapest way to make a vision model generalize better isn't a fancier architecture — it's inventing new training examples by changing the ones you have in ways that don't change the answer. A flipped photo of a cat is still a cat."},{"f":"lessons/track-15-cnns-vision/04-transfer-learning.html","t":"Transfer learning: stand on a trained network's shoulders","tn":"Cnns Vision","k":"15","x":"💡 Intuition: hire an experienced eye, teach it your job · 🛠️ From scratch: freeze, then train a small head · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Almost nobody trains a vision model from scratch. The features a network learns on a huge dataset — edges, textures, shapes — are generic, so you reuse them and train only a small piece on your own handful of labels. It's the most practical trick in applied deep learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-16-sequence-models/00-rnn.html","t":"Recurrent networks: giving a model a memory","tn":"Sequence Models","k":"16","x":"💡 Intuition: reading a book one word at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: the whole loop · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"An MLP takes a fixed-size vector; a CNN takes a fixed-size grid. But language, audio, and sensor readings arrive as sequences of varying length, where order is everything. The recurrent network's answer: read one item at a time and carry a memory forward."},{"f":"lessons/track-16-sequence-models/01-vanishing-gradients.html","t":"Why RNNs forget: vanishing & exploding gradients","tn":"Sequence Models","k":"16","x":"💡 Intuition: the compound-interest trap · 🛠️ From scratch: watch it happen · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The plain RNN has a fatal flaw on long sequences. It isn't a coding bug — it's arithmetic. Multiply many similar numbers together and the result races to zero or to infinity. That product is exactly what training an RNN computes."},{"f":"lessons/track-16-sequence-models/02-lstm.html","t":"LSTM: a memory the gradient can travel","tn":"Sequence Models","k":"16","x":"💡 Intuition: a conveyor belt with three valves · 🛠️ From scratch: the update, in two lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The LSTM solved long-term memory with one elegant idea: a memory lane that updates by addition, not by repeated multiplication — so the learning signal has a highway to travel back on. Small \"gates\" decide what to keep, what to write, and what to read."},{"f":"lessons/track-16-sequence-models/03-gru.html","t":"GRU: the same idea, with fewer parts","tn":"Sequence Models","k":"16","x":"💡 Intuition: one dial that fades old into new · 🛠️ From scratch: two gates, one state · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The GRU keeps the LSTM's key insight — gates that protect a memory — but trims it down: two gates instead of three, one state instead of two. Fewer parameters, faster training, and often just as accurate. A lesson in cutting to the essentials."},{"f":"lessons/track-16-sequence-models/04-seq2seq.html","t":"Sequence-to-sequence: from one answer to a whole output","tn":"Sequence Models","k":"16","x":"💡 Intuition: read it all, then retell it from memory · 🛠️ From scratch: encode once, decode step by step · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Translation, summarization, and speech-to-text turn one sequence into another. The encoder–decoder design does this by squeezing the whole input into a single \"context\" vector and unrolling it back out — and the weakness of that squeeze is exactly what led to attention and the Transformer."},{"f":"lessons/track-17-learning-paradigms/00-learning-paradigms.html","t":"Learning without a full answer key","tn":"Learning Paradigms","k":"17","x":"💡 Intuition: the answer key is expensive, the questions are free · 📚 The five paradigms, precisely · 🎯 The simplest case: finding groups with k-means · 🛠️ From scratch: k-means in a dozen lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Everything you have built so far learned from labelled examples — an input paired with the right answer. But most data in the world has no answers attached. This track is about the family of methods that learn anyway, and it starts by mapping the whole family and meeting its simplest member."},{"f":"lessons/track-17-learning-paradigms/01-self-supervised.html","t":"Self-supervised learning: labels for free","tn":"Learning Paradigms","k":"17","x":"💡 Intuition: to fill in the blank, you must understand the sentence · 🛠️ From scratch: an autoencoder's forward pass · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The trick that powers modern AI: instead of paying humans for answers, hide part of each input and train the model to predict the missing part from the rest. The data becomes its own teacher — and the representation it learns transfers to real tasks."},{"f":"lessons/track-17-learning-paradigms/02-contrastive.html","t":"Contrastive learning: understanding by comparison","tn":"Learning Paradigms","k":"17","x":"💡 Intuition: two photos of your friend vs a stranger · 🛠️ From scratch: the contrastive objective · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Instead of rebuilding an input, learn by comparing. Pull two altered views of the same thing together in representation space, push different things apart — and with no labels at all, a map of \"what is similar to what\" emerges. This is the engine behind SimCLR and CLIP."},{"f":"lessons/track-17-learning-paradigms/03-semi-supervised.html","t":"Semi-supervised learning: a few labels go a long way","tn":"Learning Paradigms","k":"17","x":"💡 Intuition: rumours spread through a crowd · 🛠️ From scratch: label propagation · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The realistic middle ground. You rarely have zero labels or a full answer key — you have a handful of labels and a mountain of unlabelled data. Semi-supervised learning uses the shape of the unlabelled mountain to stretch those few labels across everything."},{"f":"lessons/track-17-learning-paradigms/04-active-learning.html","t":"Active learning: let the model choose what to label","tn":"Learning Paradigms","k":"17","x":"💡 Intuition: study the questions you're unsure about · 🛠️ From scratch: the uncertainty-sampling loop · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"When every label costs money, don't label at random. Let the model point at the few examples it is most confused about, label those, and reach high accuracy with a fraction of the labelling budget. The learner becomes an active participant in its own education."},{"f":"lessons/track-18-federated-privacy/00-federated-averaging.html","t":"Federated averaging: training without pooling data","tn":"Federated Privacy","k":"18","x":"💡 Intuition: study groups that never share notes · 🛠️ From scratch: FedAvg · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Your phone's keyboard learns from your typing, a hospital's model learns from its patients — but the raw data can't be collected in one place. Federated learning trains a shared model anyway: each device trains locally and only the weights are combined."},{"f":"lessons/track-18-federated-privacy/01-non-iid.html","t":"The non-IID challenge: when clients don't look alike","tn":"Federated Privacy","k":"18","x":"💡 Intuition: experts who each saw only part of the picture · 🛠️ From scratch: measuring the non-IID penalty · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"FedAvg matched centralized training — but only because every client's data looked the same. Real clients hold wildly different data, and that skew makes their locally-trained models pull in different directions, so averaging them degrades. This is the central obstacle of federated learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-18-federated-privacy/02-differential-privacy.html","t":"Differential privacy: a mathematical privacy guarantee","tn":"Federated Privacy","k":"18","x":"💡 Intuition: a poll where everyone can plausibly deny · 🛠️ From scratch: the Laplace mechanism · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Keeping data on-device isn't enough — released results can still betray who was in the data. Differential privacy gives a provable promise: the output looks essentially the same whether or not any single person participated, so no one can tell if you were there."},{"f":"lessons/track-18-federated-privacy/03-dp-sgd.html","t":"DP-SGD: training a model that can't memorize you","tn":"Federated Privacy","k":"18","x":"💡 Intuition: cap every vote, then add static · 🛠️ From scratch: DP-SGD's two extra lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Differential privacy protected a single statistic. DP-SGD makes an entire trained neural network differentially private, with two small changes to ordinary training: clip each example's gradient, then add noise. The model learns the pattern without memorizing any individual."},{"f":"lessons/track-18-federated-privacy/04-secure-aggregation.html","t":"Secure aggregation: the server sees only the sum","tn":"Federated Privacy","k":"18","x":"💡 Intuition: secret numbers that cancel · 🛠️ From scratch: pairwise masks · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"In FedAvg the server sees each client's individual update — which can leak private data. Secure aggregation uses a beautiful cryptographic trick: clients add pairwise masks that cancel out, so the server learns only the total of everyone's updates, never any single one."},{"f":"lessons/track-19-meta-learning/00-few-shot-learning.html","t":"Few-shot learning: recognizing from a handful of examples","tn":"Meta Learning","k":"19","x":"💡 Intuition: matching against a few reference photos · 🛠️ From scratch: nearest-centroid few-shot · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A child sees one giraffe and knows giraffes forever. A standard deep network needs thousands of labelled giraffes. Few-shot learning closes that gap — learning new classes from just a few examples by leaning on a good representation and clever comparison."},{"f":"lessons/track-19-meta-learning/01-maml.html","t":"MAML: learning an initialization that adapts fast","tn":"Meta Learning","k":"19","x":"💡 Intuition: base camp near every summit · 🛠️ From scratch: Reptile (first-order MAML) · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"What if, instead of learning to solve one task, a model learned to learn any new task quickly? MAML meta-learns a single set of starting weights so good that a few gradient steps adapt it to a brand-new task. It's \"learning to learn,\" and it works."},{"f":"lessons/track-19-meta-learning/02-prototypical-networks.html","t":"Prototypical networks: few-shot by nearest prototype","tn":"Meta Learning","k":"19","x":"💡 Intuition: one representative per class · 🛠️ From scratch: prototypical classification · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"MAML adapts with gradient steps. Prototypical networks take a simpler, elegant route: learn an embedding where each class is just the average of its examples, and you classify by nearest prototype. Simple, fast, and a remarkably strong few-shot method."},{"f":"lessons/track-19-meta-learning/03-catastrophic-forgetting.html","t":"Catastrophic forgetting: the network that erases itself","tn":"Meta Learning","k":"19","x":"💡 Intuition: one whiteboard, two lessons · 🛠️ From scratch: demonstrating the collapse · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Teach a neural network task A, then task B, and something alarming happens: it forgets task A almost completely. This \"catastrophic forgetting\" is the central obstacle to building machines that learn continuously, the way humans do."},{"f":"lessons/track-19-meta-learning/04-continual-learning.html","t":"Continual learning: learning without forgetting","tn":"Meta Learning","k":"19","x":"💡 Intuition: study old flashcards while learning new ones · 🛠️ From scratch: experience replay · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Catastrophic forgetting has fixes. The simplest and most effective is replay — rehearse a few old examples while learning the new task, and the old knowledge survives. This lesson builds it, sketches its main rival (EWC), and closes the track on lifelong learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-2-optimization/00-derivatives-gradients.html","t":"Derivatives & gradients, geometrically","tn":"Optimization","k":"2","x":"💡 Intuition: the slope under your feet · 🛠️ From scratch: gradients you can check numerically · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Training a model means rolling downhill on a loss surface. Before we can descend, we need the one tool that tells us which way is down and how steep it is: the derivative in 1D, and its multivariable big sibling, the gradient."},{"f":"lessons/track-2-optimization/01-gradient-descent.html","t":"Gradient descent from scratch","tn":"Optimization","k":"2","x":"💡 Intuition: a blindfolded walk downhill · 🛠️ From scratch: descend, then fit a real model · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One line — \"step a little way downhill, then repeat\" — is how essentially every model in modern AI is trained. Here we derive it, watch it work, learn its one critical knob, and use it to fit a real model end to end."},{"f":"lessons/track-2-optimization/02-convexity.html","t":"Convexity & why it matters","tn":"Optimization","k":"2","x":"💡 Intuition: one bowl, or an egg carton · 🛠️ From scratch: test convexity, then see it decide the outcome · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"When is \"roll downhill\" guaranteed to find the best answer, not just a mediocre one? The answer is convexity — the single property that separates optimization problems we can fully trust from the wild, wrinkled landscapes of deep learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-2-optimization/03-momentum-adam.html","t":"Momentum, RMSProp & Adam","tn":"Optimization","k":"2","x":"💡 Intuition: give the ball weight, and a per-direction gearbox · 🛠️ From scratch: four optimizers on one hard bowl · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Plain gradient descent is honest but easily frustrated — it crawls down long shallow valleys and rattles across steep ones. Three upgrades fix this, culminating in Adam, the optimizer that trains most of modern deep learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-2-optimization/04-newton-second-order.html","t":"Newton's method: using the shape of the bowl","tn":"Optimization","k":"2","x":"💡 Intuition: if you know the bowl is a parabola, jump to its bottom · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Gradient descent knows only which way is downhill. Newton's method also knows how the ground curves — and with that extra information it can often leap straight to the bottom. It's too expensive to use directly on big networks, but its idea hides inside every optimizer you'll use."},{"f":"lessons/track-20-graph-neural-networks/00-graphs-as-data.html","t":"Graphs: data that comes as a web of connections","tn":"Graph Neural Networks","k":"20","x":"💡 Intuition: a table of who-connects-to-whom · 🛠️ From scratch: a graph and its matrices · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"An image is a grid, a sentence is a line, but a social network, a molecule, a road map, and the web are none of those — they are graphs : things joined by relationships. To learn on them, we first need to write a graph down as numbers a model can chew on."},{"f":"lessons/track-20-graph-neural-networks/01-message-passing.html","t":"Message passing: how information moves on a graph","tn":"Graph Neural Networks","k":"20","x":"💡 Intuition: gossip through a social network · 🛠️ From scratch: message passing is one multiply · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One idea sits underneath every graph neural network: each node updates itself by gathering messages from its neighbours. Do it once and a node knows its immediate surroundings; repeat and news travels across the whole graph. This is the beating heart of the track."},{"f":"lessons/track-20-graph-neural-networks/02-gcn.html","t":"Graph Convolutional Networks: message passing that learns","tn":"Graph Neural Networks","k":"20","x":"💡 Intuition: convolution, but on a graph · 🛠️ From scratch: a 2-layer GCN · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Take message passing, insert a weight matrix and a nonlinearity, stack two layers — and you have a GCN, the workhorse of graph learning. Train it on a handful of labelled nodes and the graph structure fills in the rest."},{"f":"lessons/track-20-graph-neural-networks/03-gat.html","t":"Graph Attention Networks: learning which neighbours matter","tn":"Graph Neural Networks","k":"20","x":"💡 Intuition: not all neighbours are equally worth hearing · 🛠️ From scratch: the GAT attention layer · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A GCN treats every neighbour as equally important. But some neighbours are signal and some are noise. A GAT learns a weight for each neighbour — the same attention idea that powers Transformers, now on a graph — so a node can lean on the neighbours that actually help."},{"f":"lessons/track-20-graph-neural-networks/04-graph-classification.html","t":"Graph-level tasks: labelling a whole graph","tn":"Graph Neural Networks","k":"20","x":"💡 Intuition: from many opinions to one verdict · 🛠️ From scratch: readout + classify · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"So far we labelled individual nodes. But the biggest wins — is this molecule a drug? is this transaction ring a fraud? — need a label for the entire graph. The trick is to pool many node embeddings into one graph vector, and it's how GNNs are quietly reshaping chemistry, medicine, and search."},{"f":"lessons/track-21-bayesian-ml/00-bayesian-inference.html","t":"Bayesian inference: updating beliefs with evidence","tn":"Bayesian Ml","k":"21","x":"💡 Intuition: how a detective updates a theory · 🛠️ From scratch: Bayesian updating is counting · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Most models give you a single answer. A Bayesian model gives you a whole distribution of plausible answers — and updates it as evidence arrives. It's a principled machine for reasoning under uncertainty, and it starts with one line: posterior ∝ prior × likelihood."},{"f":"lessons/track-21-bayesian-ml/01-mcmc.html","t":"MCMC: sampling a distribution you can't solve","tn":"Bayesian Ml","k":"21","x":"💡 Intuition: a hiker exploring hills in the fog · 🛠️ From scratch: Metropolis–Hastings · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Conjugacy gave us a tidy formula, but almost no real posterior has one. Markov Chain Monte Carlo draws samples from any distribution you can evaluate — even up to an unknown constant — by taking a clever random walk that spends time in each region in proportion to its probability."},{"f":"lessons/track-21-bayesian-ml/02-variational-inference.html","t":"Variational inference: turn inference into optimization","tn":"Bayesian Ml","k":"21","x":"💡 Intuition: the best-fitting simple shape · 🛠️ From scratch: maximize the ELBO · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"MCMC samples the posterior but can be slow. Variational inference takes the opposite bet: pick a simple distribution and adjust it until it hugs the posterior as closely as possible. Inference becomes optimization — fast, scalable, and the workhorse of Bayesian deep learning."},{"f":"lessons/track-21-bayesian-ml/03-gaussian-processes.html","t":"Gaussian processes: distributions over functions","tn":"Bayesian Ml","k":"21","x":"💡 Intuition: connect the dots, but honestly · 🛠️ From scratch: GP regression · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Bayesian inference over parameters is powerful, but a Gaussian process goes further — it puts a probability distribution over entire functions . The result is a regression that fits smoothly through your data and, crucially, reports growing uncertainty exactly where it has none."},{"f":"lessons/track-21-bayesian-ml/04-calibration.html","t":"Uncertainty & calibration: can you trust the confidence?","tn":"Bayesian Ml","k":"21","x":"💡 Intuition: the weather forecaster's test · 🛠️ From scratch: measure and fix calibration · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A model that says \"99% sure\" and is right 99% of the time is calibrated. Most modern models are not — they're overconfident. This lesson shows how to measure that gap and fix it with one number, so a model's confidence actually means what it says."},{"f":"lessons/track-22-causal-inference/00-correlation-vs-causation.html","t":"Correlation vs causation: why prediction isn't enough","tn":"Causal Inference","k":"22","x":"💡 Intuition: the third variable pulling the strings · 🛠️ From scratch: exposing a spurious correlation · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Ice-cream sales predict drownings — but banning ice cream won't save anyone. Machine learning is brilliant at finding correlations, and correlations are exactly the wrong tool for deciding what to do . This track is about the missing piece: causation."},{"f":"lessons/track-22-causal-inference/01-confounding.html","t":"Confounding: when a real effect flips its sign","tn":"Causal Inference","k":"22","x":"💡 Intuition: comparing groups that aren't comparable · 🛠️ From scratch: manufacturing (and resolving) the paradox · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A confounder doesn't just fake correlations — it can make a treatment that helps everyone look actively harmful. Simpson's paradox is confounding at its most alarming, and it shows why you must think about causal structure before trusting any comparison."},{"f":"lessons/track-22-causal-inference/02-the-do-operator.html","t":"The do-operator: the difference between seeing and doing","tn":"Causal Inference","k":"22","x":"💡 Intuition: cut the strings, then pull the lever · 🛠️ From scratch: simulating an intervention · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Ordinary probability can express \"seeing\" — what's likely given an observation. Causal questions are about \"doing\" — what happens if we intervene. The do-operator is the notation that finally tells them apart, and it's the mathematical heart of causal inference."},{"f":"lessons/track-22-causal-inference/03-backdoor-adjustment.html","t":"Backdoor adjustment: computing do() from data","tn":"Causal Inference","k":"22","x":"💡 Intuition: close the side door · 🛠️ From scratch: backdoor adjustment · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"You usually can't run the experiment. Backdoor adjustment is the recipe that recovers the causal effect — the do() answer — from ordinary observational data, by adjusting for exactly the right confounders and no others."},{"f":"lessons/track-22-causal-inference/04-estimating-treatment-effects.html","t":"Estimating treatment effects: propensity & reweighting","tn":"Causal Inference","k":"22","x":"💡 Intuition: reweight to rebalance · 🛠️ From scratch: inverse propensity weighting · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Real confounders are continuous, not neat categories, so you can't just stratify. Propensity scores and inverse-propensity weighting rebalance a confounded dataset into a pseudo-experiment — and recover the average treatment effect."},{"f":"lessons/track-23-efficient-ml/00-knowledge-distillation.html","t":"Knowledge distillation: teaching a small model to think big","tn":"Efficient Ml","k":"23","x":"💡 Intuition: learn from the teacher's hesitation · 🛠️ From scratch: distillation · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The best models are enormous and expensive to run. Distillation trains a small, cheap \"student\" to mimic a big \"teacher\" — not just its answers, but its full confidence over every class — capturing most of the accuracy at a fraction of the cost."},{"f":"lessons/track-23-efficient-ml/01-pruning.html","t":"Pruning: most of a network is dead weight","tn":"Efficient Ml","k":"23","x":"💡 Intuition: trim the branches that bear no fruit · 🛠️ From scratch: magnitude pruning · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A surprising fact about trained neural networks: you can delete the majority of their weights — often 70% or more — and accuracy barely changes. Pruning exploits this massive redundancy to make models smaller and faster."},{"f":"lessons/track-23-efficient-ml/02-quantization.html","t":"Quantization: fewer bits per weight","tn":"Efficient Ml","k":"23","x":"💡 Intuition: round to a coarser ruler · 🛠️ From scratch: uniform quantization · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Neural networks store weights as 32-bit floating-point numbers — but that precision is wildly excessive. Quantization rounds each weight to a handful of levels (8 bits, or fewer), cutting memory by 4× or more and enabling fast integer math, with almost no accuracy loss."},{"f":"lessons/track-23-efficient-ml/03-parallelism.html","t":"Data & model parallelism: training on many machines","tn":"Efficient Ml","k":"23","x":"💡 Intuition: divide the work, combine the results · 🛠️ From scratch: data parallelism · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Modern models are trained on thousands of GPUs. Data parallelism splits the batch across workers and averages their gradients; model parallelism splits one giant model across devices. Both let you train what no single machine could."},{"f":"lessons/track-23-efficient-ml/04-mixed-precision.html","t":"Mixed precision: training in 16 bits, safely","tn":"Efficient Ml","k":"23","x":"💡 Intuition: turn up the volume before recording · 🛠️ From scratch: loss scaling · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Training in 16-bit floating point instead of 32-bit halves memory and roughly doubles speed on modern hardware. But 16-bit numbers can't represent tiny gradients, which silently vanish. One clever trick — loss scaling — fixes it, and mixed precision became the default for training large models."},{"f":"lessons/track-24-frontier-agents/00-mixture-of-experts.html","t":"Mixture of Experts: huge models that run cheaply","tn":"Frontier Agents","k":"24","x":"💡 Intuition: a receptionist routing to specialists · 🛠️ From scratch: the routed mixture · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"How do you build a model with a trillion parameters without paying to run a trillion parameters on every input? Mixture of Experts: many specialist sub-networks and a router that activates only a few per input — enormous capacity, small compute."},{"f":"lessons/track-24-frontier-agents/01-state-space-models.html","t":"State-space models: linear-time sequences","tn":"Frontier Agents","k":"24","x":"💡 Intuition: a running summary instead of re-reading · 🛠️ From scratch: the linear-recurrence scan · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Transformers are powerful but their attention costs grow with the square of the sequence length — brutal for long inputs. State-space models like Mamba process sequences in linear time with a running state, while still remembering far into the past."},{"f":"lessons/track-24-frontier-agents/02-multimodal-models.html","t":"Multimodal models: one space for images and text","tn":"Frontier Agents","k":"24","x":"💡 Intuition: a universal translator between seeing and reading · 🛠️ From scratch: contrastive alignment + zero-shot · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Models like CLIP learn images and language in a single shared space, so a picture of a dog and the words \"a dog\" land in the same place. That one idea enables zero-shot classification and powers the text-to-image generators behind modern AI art."},{"f":"lessons/track-24-frontier-agents/03-llm-agents.html","t":"LLM agents & tool use: from predicting text to taking action","tn":"Frontier Agents","k":"24","x":"💡 Intuition: a smart person with a phone and a calculator · 🛠️ From scratch: a tool-using agent · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A language model alone can only predict text — it can't reliably do arithmetic, look up live facts, or run code. An agent wraps the model in a loop where it reasons, calls tools, observes results, and acts — turning a text predictor into a system that gets things done."},{"f":"lessons/track-24-frontier-agents/04-planning-and-memory.html","t":"Planning & memory: agents that tackle the long haul","tn":"Frontier Agents","k":"24","x":"💡 Intuition: think before you leap, and take notes · 🛠️ From scratch: planning with memory · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A single reason-act loop handles simple tasks. But truly complex, multi-step goals need more: the ability to plan ahead — search for a strategy before acting — and to remember across many steps. These are what turn a reactive agent into a capable one, and they close the course."},{"f":"lessons/track-3-ml-theory/00-generalization.html","t":"The learning problem & generalization","tn":"Ml Theory","k":"3","x":"💡 Intuition: the student who memorized the practice exam · 📐 Making it precise: two errors and the gap between them · 🛠️ From scratch: watch the U with your own eyes · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Track 2 taught us to drive the training loss to zero. Track 3 asks the deeper question: so what? A model that aces its training data but fails on new data has learned nothing. Generalization — performing well on data you’ve never seen — is the whole point."},{"f":"lessons/track-3-ml-theory/01-bias-variance.html","t":"Bias–variance decomposition","tn":"Ml Theory","k":"3","x":"💡 Intuition: darts at a dartboard · 📐 The decomposition, one term at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: measuring bias and variance · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The U-shaped test-error curve isn’t magic — it’s the sum of two competing quantities pulling in opposite directions. Splitting error into bias and variance is the single most clarifying lens in all of machine-learning theory."},{"f":"lessons/track-3-ml-theory/02-regularization.html","t":"Regularization (L1/L2), geometrically","tn":"Ml Theory","k":"3","x":"💡 Intuition: a leash on the weights · 📐 Two penalties, one objective · 🛠️ From scratch: ridge in one line, lasso by soft-thresholding · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"If overfitting is a model using too much freedom to chase noise, the cure is to take some freedom away. Regularization does this by penalizing large weights — and the shape of the penalty explains one of ML’s most useful tricks: automatic feature selection."},{"f":"lessons/track-3-ml-theory/03-pac-vc.html","t":"PAC learning & VC dimension: how much data is enough?","tn":"Ml Theory","k":"3","x":"💡 Intuition: \"probably approximately correct\" · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"When can you trust that a model which fits your training data will also work on new data? Learning theory gives a real answer — one that ties together model complexity, dataset size, and the gap between training and true performance."},{"f":"lessons/track-4-deep-learning/00-neuron-graph.html","t":"The neuron & the computational graph","tn":"Deep Learning","k":"4","x":"💡 Intuition: a neuron is a \"should I?\" decision · 📐 The neuron, one symbol at a time · 🧩 The computational graph: one big formula, many tiny steps · 🛠️ From scratch: a neuron and a tiny graph · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Everything you've built — vectors, dot products, gradients, loss — now snaps together into the atom of deep learning: the neuron. And the way we'll string neurons into trainable networks is an idea of startling generality: the computational graph."},{"f":"lessons/track-4-deep-learning/01-backpropagation.html","t":"Backpropagation by hand","tn":"Deep Learning","k":"4","x":"💡 Intuition: passing \"blame\" back down the line · 📐 The chain rule, walked backward · 🧮 Worked example: backprop on \\(d = a\\times b + c\\) · 🛠️ From scratch: backprop in ten lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Backpropagation has a fearsome reputation it does not deserve. It is nothing more than the chain rule from Track 2, applied backward through the computational graph — and it computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every parameter in a single sweep."},{"f":"lessons/track-4-deep-learning/02-autograd-engine.html","t":"Build an autograd engine","tn":"Deep Learning","k":"4","x":"💡 Intuition: numbers that keep a receipt · 📐 A value that remembers: the four fields · 🛠️ The engine, built up piece by piece · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"This is the lesson the whole curriculum has been building toward. In about sixty lines of Python you'll write a real automatic-differentiation engine — the same machinery, in miniature, that powers PyTorch and every model trained today. Once you've built it, deep learning stops being magic."},{"f":"lessons/track-4-deep-learning/03-mlp-activations.html","t":"MLPs, activations & initialization: the bend that makes depth work","tn":"Deep Learning","k":"4","x":"💡 Intuition: why you need a bend · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Stack linear layers and you get… another linear layer — no matter how many you use. The one ingredient that makes deep networks powerful is a simple nonlinear \"bend\" between layers. Choosing it, and starting the weights right, is what makes training actually work."},{"f":"lessons/track-4-deep-learning/04-training-loop.html","t":"The training loop: four steps that train everything","tn":"Deep Learning","k":"4","x":"💡 Intuition: predict, grade, blame, nudge · 🛠️ From scratch: the loop in eight lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Every model in this course — every model in modern AI — is trained by the same tiny cycle repeated millions of times: predict, measure the error, find the blame, nudge the weights. Here we assemble it and train a real network end to end."},{"f":"lessons/track-5-transformers/00-sequences-attention.html","t":"From sequences to attention","tn":"Transformers","k":"5","x":"💡 Intuition: every word gets to look around the room · 🛠️ From scratch: attention weights in eight lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Language is a sequence , and the meaning of a word depends on the other words around it — \"it\" points back to something said earlier. The transformer's answer is one of the most consequential ideas in modern AI: instead of passing meaning down a relay line, let every word look directly at every other word and decide, for itself, which ones matter."},{"f":"lessons/track-5-transformers/01-self-attention.html","t":"Self-attention, step by step","tn":"Transformers","k":"5","x":"💡 Intuition: a party of questions and name tags · 🛠️ From scratch: scaled dot-product attention · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Attention sounds mysterious until you see it is three dot products in a trench coat. Every token asks a question, every token wears a label, and the answer each token walks away with is a weighted blend of what the others carry — all built from the linear algebra of Track 1."},{"f":"lessons/track-5-transformers/02-multihead-block.html","t":"Multi-head attention & the transformer block","tn":"Transformers","k":"5","x":"💡 Intuition: a panel of specialists reading the same sentence · 🧱 Two tricks that make deep stacks trainable · 🛠️ From scratch: multi-head and the block · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"One attention head captures one kind of relationship. Real language has many at once — grammar, reference, topic. The fix is several heads in parallel, wrapped with two ideas that make deep stacks trainable: residual connections and normalization. Together they form the transformer block you will stack into a GPT."},{"f":"lessons/track-5-transformers/03-positional-encoding.html","t":"Positional encodings","tn":"Transformers","k":"5","x":"💡 Intuition: give every word a \"where am I\" stamp · 🛠️ From scratch: sinusoidal positions · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Attention has a surprising blind spot: it treats a sentence as a bag of words, with no sense of order. \"Dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" look identical to it. Positional encodings fix this by stamping each token with where it sits — restoring the sequence to the sequence model."},{"f":"lessons/track-5-transformers/04-build-gpt.html","t":"Build & train a small GPT","tn":"Transformers","k":"5","x":"💡 Intuition: a very good autocomplete · 🛠️ From scratch: the whole GPT, forward and generate · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Everything converges here. Embeddings, attention, multi-head, blocks, positions, the training loop — assemble them and you have a GPT. You will train a real one, from scratch, that learns to generate text. The architecture behind ChatGPT, demystified into code you wrote yourself."},{"f":"lessons/track-6-generative/00-what-is-generative.html","t":"What is a generative model?","tn":"Generative","k":"6","x":"💡 Intuition: the critic and the chef · 🧠 The concept, one step at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: fit, then sample · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Every model you have built so far answers a question about data: which class, what value. A generative model does something stranger and more powerful — it learns what the data is , well enough to dream up brand-new examples that look like they belong."},{"f":"lessons/track-6-generative/01-autoregressive.html","t":"Autoregressive models","tn":"Generative","k":"6","x":"💡 Intuition: writing a sentence one word at a time · 🧠 The concept, one step at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: ancestral sampling in a loop · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The most direct way to model \\(p(x)\\): break it into a chain of one-step predictions, each conditioned on everything generated so far. It is exact, it is simple, and you have already trained one — GPT."},{"f":"lessons/track-6-generative/02-vae.html","t":"Variational autoencoders","tn":"Generative","k":"6","x":"💡 Intuition: a sketch artist and their notes · 🧠 The concept, one step at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: the VAE loss · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Compress data to a small, well-behaved latent code and learn to decode it back. Make that code probabilistic and you get a generative model you can sample from — trained with one of the most elegant objectives in machine learning, the ELBO."},{"f":"lessons/track-6-generative/03-gan.html","t":"Generative adversarial networks","tn":"Generative","k":"6","x":"💡 Intuition: the forger and the detective · 🧠 The concept, one step at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: one adversarial round · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Throw away likelihood entirely. Instead, pit a forger against a detective: the generator learns to produce data so convincing that a discriminator cannot tell it from real. When the detective is reduced to guessing, the forgeries are good."},{"f":"lessons/track-6-generative/04-diffusion.html","t":"Diffusion models","tn":"Generative","k":"6","x":"💡 Intuition: un-dissolving a drop of ink · 🧠 The concept, one step at a time · 🛠️ From scratch: train by predicting noise, sample by denoising · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Destroy data with noise in many small, fixed steps — then train a network to reverse one step of that destruction. Generation becomes a walk from pure noise back to data. This is the engine behind today's best image generators, and it trains with a loss as simple as \"predict the noise.\""},{"f":"lessons/track-7-rl/00-mdps.html","t":"MDPs & the reinforcement-learning problem","tn":"Rl","k":"7","x":"💡 Intuition: teaching a dog a trick · 📐 The pieces: agent, environment, reward · 🛠️ From scratch: an MDP is just a step function · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Every model so far learned from a fixed dataset. Reinforcement learning is different: an agent acts , the world responds, and it must learn — from reward alone — how to behave. First we need the language to state that problem precisely."},{"f":"lessons/track-7-rl/01-value-q-learning.html","t":"Value iteration & Q-learning","tn":"Rl","k":"7","x":"💡 Intuition: ripples spreading from the goal · 📐 The Bellman optimality equation · 🛠️ From scratch: both algorithms in 30 lines · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"If you knew the value of every state, acting well would be trivial — just step toward higher value. This lesson builds that value map two ways: value iteration when the rules are known, and Q-learning when they aren't."},{"f":"lessons/track-7-rl/02-policy-gradients.html","t":"Policy gradients (REINFORCE)","tn":"Rl","k":"7","x":"💡 Intuition: keep more of what worked · 📐 The policy gradient theorem (the log-derivative trick) · 🛠️ From scratch: REINFORCE on a grid world · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Instead of learning how good every action is and then acting greedily, what if we adjusted the policy directly — nudging its knobs to make rewarding behaviour more likely? That is policy gradient, and it powers everything from robot control to aligning ChatGPT."},{"f":"lessons/track-7-rl/03-actor-critic-ppo.html","t":"Actor–critic & PPO","tn":"Rl","k":"7","x":"💡 Intuition: a player and a coach · 📐 Actor, critic, and the advantage · 🛠️ From scratch: actor–critic, plus the PPO clip · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"REINFORCE works but wobbles — the full random return is a noisy teacher. Actor–critic hands the policy a coach that grades each move instantly, and PPO makes the whole thing stable enough to train robots and language models."},{"f":"lessons/track-7-rl/04-rlhf.html","t":"RLHF: aligning language models","tn":"Rl","k":"7","x":"💡 Intuition: a critic who learns your taste · 📐 Stage 1: a reward model from preferences · 📐 Stage 2: optimise the policy, but stay close to home · 🛠️ From scratch: the two equations that are RLHF · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"You can't write a formula for \"helpful, honest, and harmless\" — but you can usually say which of two answers is better. RLHF turns those human comparisons into a reward, then uses the PPO you just built to steer a language model toward it."},{"f":"lessons/track-8-papers/00-how-to-read.html","t":"How to read a paper (the three-pass method)","tn":"Papers","k":"8","x":"💡 Intuition: scouting a hiking trail · 🛠️ A reusable notes template · 🔑 Key ideas to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Reading a research paper front-to-back like a novel is the slowest, most painful way to do it. Skilled researchers read in three quick passes, each with one job — decide if it's worth your time, grasp the main idea, then rebuild it in full — so you never spend four hours on a paper you should have dropped in five minutes."},{"f":"lessons/track-8-papers/01-reproducing.html","t":"Reproducing results faithfully","tn":"Papers","k":"8","x":"💡 Intuition: a recipe that comes out flat · 🛠️ Make your run repeatable · 🔑 Key ideas to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Reading tells you what a paper claims; reproducing tells you whether you can actually get the same number. When your run says 89% and the paper says 92%, the gap isn't failure — it's a list of clues. Reproduction is the detective work that turns a claim into something you trust and can build on."},{"f":"lessons/track-8-papers/02-critiquing.html","t":"Critiquing claims & evidence","tn":"Papers","k":"8","x":"💡 Intuition: a detective checking an alibi · 🛠️ Check a claim in five lines · 🔑 Key ideas to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A result is only as good as its evidence. The most important reading skill is separating what a paper showed from what it claimed — and learning to spot the handful of ways results routinely mislead: no fair baseline, a cherry-picked seed, a p-value fished out of many tries."},{"f":"lessons/track-9-methods/00-designing-experiments.html","t":"Designing experiments","tn":"Methods","k":"9","x":"💡 Intuition: a fair taste test · 🛠️ A controlled experiment loop · 🔑 Key ideas to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"A result you can trust is built long before the code runs — in the design. A good experiment changes exactly one thing, holds everything else fixed, writes down what it expects in advance, and runs enough times to tell a real effect from random luck."},{"f":"lessons/track-9-methods/01-ablations.html","t":"Ablations & controls","tn":"Methods","k":"9","x":"💡 Intuition: muting instruments in a song · 🛠️ An ablation loop · 🔑 Key ideas to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Your method has five clever parts and it hits 94%. But which part is doing the work? An ablation answers that — by switching off one component at a time and measuring what breaks. It's the difference between \"it works\" and \"I understand why it works.\""},{"f":"lessons/track-9-methods/02-honest-evaluation.html","t":"Honest evaluation: how good results lie to you","tn":"Methods","k":"9","x":"💡 Intuition: the exam you accidentally saw beforehand · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"The easiest person to fool is yourself. A model can score 99% and be worthless — if the test was contaminated, the metric was wrong, or you peeked. Honest evaluation is the discipline of measuring performance you can actually trust."},{"f":"lessons/track-9-methods/03-writing.html","t":"Writing the paper: turning results into a story others build on","tn":"Methods","k":"9","x":"💡 Intuition: build the paper around one claim · 🔑 Key results to remember · 🧩 Quiz · ✍️ Practice","s":"Brilliant work that no one understands changes nothing. A paper's job isn't to list what you did — it's to make one clear claim, prove it, and hand the reader everything they need to believe and reuse it. Writing is a research skill, not an afterthought."}],"glossary":[{"term":"Vector","desc":"An ordered list of numbers; a point or arrow in space. The basic unit of data in ML."},{"term":"Dot product","desc":"Multiply matching entries of two vectors and add them up; measures how aligned they are (0 = perpendicular)."},{"term":"Matrix","desc":"A grid of numbers that represents a linear transformation — it rotates, scales, or shears vectors."},{"term":"Eigenvector","desc":"A special direction a matrix only stretches (never rotates); its stretch factor is the eigenvalue."},{"term":"Bayes' rule","desc":"Updates a belief with evidence: posterior ∝ prior × likelihood."},{"term":"Expectation","desc":"The long-run average value of a random quantity, weighting each outcome by its probability."},{"term":"Variance","desc":"How spread out a random quantity is around its mean."},{"term":"Gradient","desc":"The vector of slopes of a function; it points in the direction of steepest increase."},{"term":"Gradient descent","desc":"Repeatedly step downhill (opposite the gradient) to minimize a loss."},{"term":"Learning rate","desc":"The step size in gradient descent; too small is slow, too large diverges."},{"term":"Convex function","desc":"A bowl-shaped function with a single global minimum — easy to optimize."},{"term":"Momentum","desc":"Accumulate past gradients so optimization rolls through small bumps and ravines faster."},{"term":"Adam","desc":"A popular optimizer that adapts the step size per parameter using running averages of gradients."},{"term":"Entropy","desc":"The average surprise (in bits) of a distribution; how uncertain it is."},{"term":"Cross-entropy","desc":"The average bits to encode data from p using a model q; the standard classification loss."},{"term":"KL divergence","desc":"The extra bits wasted using code q instead of the true p; ≥0, and not symmetric."},{"term":"Mutual information","desc":"How many bits knowing one variable removes from your uncertainty about another."},{"term":"Generalization","desc":"How well a model does on new, unseen data (not just its training data)."},{"term":"Overfitting","desc":"Memorizing the training data (including its noise) so test performance suffers."},{"term":"Bias–variance tradeoff","desc":"Error splits into bias (too simple) and variance (too sensitive); you balance them."},{"term":"Regularization","desc":"Penalizing complexity (e.g. L1/L2) to reduce overfitting."},{"term":"Cross-validation","desc":"Estimating test performance by rotating which slice of data is held out."},{"term":"Linear regression","desc":"Fit a straight line (or plane) by minimizing squared error."},{"term":"Logistic regression","desc":"Linear model squashed through a sigmoid to output a probability for classification."},{"term":"k-means","desc":"Unsupervised clustering: assign points to the nearest centre, move centres to the mean, repeat."},{"term":"Decision tree","desc":"A cascade of yes/no questions that splits the data into pure regions."},{"term":"Random forest","desc":"An average of many decorrelated decision trees — smoother and more robust than one."},{"term":"Neuron","desc":"A weighted sum of inputs plus a bias, passed through an activation function."},{"term":"Backpropagation","desc":"The chain rule applied backward through a network to get every weight's gradient."},{"term":"Activation function","desc":"A nonlinearity (ReLU, sigmoid, tanh) that lets networks learn curved decision boundaries."},{"term":"MLP","desc":"Multi-layer perceptron: layers of fully-connected neurons; the basic deep network."},{"term":"Dropout","desc":"Randomly zeroing neurons during training to prevent co-adaptation and overfitting."},{"term":"Convolution","desc":"Slide a small shared filter over an image to detect a local pattern everywhere."},{"term":"Pooling","desc":"Shrink a feature map by keeping the strongest (max) or average response in each window."},{"term":"Transfer learning","desc":"Reuse features learned on big data, then fine-tune a small head on your task."},{"term":"RNN","desc":"A network that processes a sequence step by step, carrying a hidden state (memory) forward."},{"term":"Vanishing gradient","desc":"Gradients shrink exponentially through many steps, so early inputs are forgotten."},{"term":"LSTM","desc":"An RNN cell with gates and a protected cell state, so gradients survive long sequences."},{"term":"Attention","desc":"Let each token gather information from every other token, weighted by relevance."},{"term":"Self-attention","desc":"Attention where queries, keys, and values all come from the same sequence."},{"term":"Transformer","desc":"An architecture built from stacked self-attention + MLP blocks; the backbone of modern AI."},{"term":"Positional encoding","desc":"Added signals that tell a Transformer the order of tokens (attention alone is order-blind)."},{"term":"Tokenization","desc":"Splitting text into subword pieces (tokens) the model can embed."},{"term":"Scaling laws","desc":"Loss falls as a predictable power law in model size, data, and compute."},{"term":"LoRA","desc":"Fine-tune by learning a tiny low-rank update instead of all the weights."},{"term":"Quantization","desc":"Store weights in fewer bits (e.g. 4-bit) to shrink memory with little quality loss."},{"term":"RAG","desc":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation: fetch relevant documents and feed them to the model as context."},{"term":"Autoregressive model","desc":"Generates a sequence one element at a time, each conditioned on all previous ones."},{"term":"VAE","desc":"Variational autoencoder: encodes data into a probabilistic latent code you can sample from."},{"term":"GAN","desc":"A generator and discriminator locked in a game until fakes look real."},{"term":"Diffusion model","desc":"Learns to reverse a gradual noising process, turning noise into data."},{"term":"Reparameterization trick","desc":"Move randomness off the gradient path so you can backprop through a sample."},{"term":"Reinforcement learning","desc":"Learn a policy by trial and error to maximize cumulative reward."},{"term":"MDP","desc":"Markov Decision Process: states, actions, transitions, rewards — the formal setting for RL."},{"term":"Q-learning","desc":"Learn the value of each action in each state, then act greedily."},{"term":"Policy gradient","desc":"Directly adjust a policy's parameters to make high-reward actions more likely."},{"term":"RLHF","desc":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback: align a model to a reward learned from human preferences."},{"term":"Self-supervised learning","desc":"Create labels from the data itself (e.g. predict a masked part) — no human labels needed."},{"term":"Contrastive learning","desc":"Pull together representations of the same thing; push apart different things."},{"term":"Active learning","desc":"Let the model request labels for the examples it's least sure about."},{"term":"Graph neural network","desc":"A network that passes messages along edges so each node aggregates from its neighbours."},{"term":"Prior / Posterior","desc":"Your belief before / after seeing data, linked by Bayes' rule."},{"term":"MCMC","desc":"Draw samples from a hard distribution by taking a cleverly biased random walk."},{"term":"Variational inference","desc":"Approximate a hard posterior by fitting a simple distribution (maximizing the ELBO)."},{"term":"Gaussian process","desc":"A distribution over functions that is confident near data and honestly uncertain far from it."},{"term":"Few-shot learning","desc":"Learn a new task from just a handful of labelled examples."},{"term":"MAML","desc":"Meta-learning that finds an initialization any new task can reach in a few gradient steps."},{"term":"Catastrophic forgetting","desc":"A network learning a new task erases what it knew about old ones."},{"term":"Federated learning","desc":"Train a shared model across devices while the data never leaves them."},{"term":"Differential privacy","desc":"Add calibrated noise so no single person's data can be identified from the output."},{"term":"Confounder","desc":"A hidden common cause that makes two things look related when neither causes the other."},{"term":"do-operator","desc":"Notation for intervening (doing) rather than merely observing — the heart of causality."},{"term":"Knowledge distillation","desc":"Train a small student model to imitate a big teacher's soft predictions."},{"term":"Mixed precision","desc":"Train using 16-bit numbers (with loss scaling) to go faster and use less memory."},{"term":"Mixture of Experts","desc":"A gate routes each input to a few of many expert sub-networks — huge capacity, small compute."},{"term":"State-space model","desc":"A linear recurrence that carries long-range memory in linear time (an attention alternative)."},{"term":"LLM agent","desc":"A model that loops: reason, act (call a tool), observe the result, and repeat."},{"term":"Ablation","desc":"Remove one component to measure how much it actually contributed."},{"term":"Alignment problem","desc":"Getting an AI to pursue what we mean, not just the proxy objective we wrote down."},{"term":"Goodhart's law","desc":"When a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure."},{"term":"Interpretability","desc":"Opening the black box to read what a model's internal parts actually compute."},{"term":"Adversarial example","desc":"A tiny, human-invisible perturbation that flips a model's prediction."}]};
